  "primary_subject_agency_ids": [],
  "summary": "Wisconsin State Rep. Calvin Callahan, chair of the Assembly Committee on Consumer Protection, announced plans to hold a January hearing on Flock ALPR cameras, citing privacy concerns, misuse, and Flock's five-year data retention and third-party data sales. Several Wisconsin law enforcement agencies, including Racine County, have discontinued Flock use, and the Wisconsin Institute for Law and Liberty is supporting a lawsuit in Waukesha County.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "What was sold to us as a targeted public safety tool has rapidly evolved into something far more invasive and sometimes out of control, with reports of misuse, mistaken stops, and access to data that should concern every Wisconsinite",
      "paragraph_idx": 6
    },
    {
      "quote": "Several law enforcement agencies in Wisconsin have discontinued use of Flock cameras and removed them after learning that the company retains data from the cameras for five years and sells the data to third parties as a \"Traffic Analytics\" product.",
      "paragraph_idx": 8
    },
    {
      "quote": "This is not what we agreed to, and it is not what the public was led to believe",
      "paragraph_idx": 9
    }
  ],
